==============================================================================
  txfu -- text processing tools
==============================================================================

txfu is a small collection of text-processing tools -- line-oriented filters
in the Unix tradition, including alternates of the usual ones such as grep and
cut, plus a few stream reducers for quick numeric summaries.

All tools are plain Perl scripts. They read from standard input or from file
arguments, write to standard output, and compose cleanly in pipelines.

AUTHOR
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2025-2026 (c) Vladi Belperchinov-Shabanski "Cade"
              https://cade.noxrun.com
                     <cade@noxrun.com>
              https://github.com/cade-vs/txfu


TOOLS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  grepw    Print lines that contain ALL of the given words (AND-grep)
  pcut     Extract / reorder fields -- an enhanced cut with sort,
           sample, and head/tail
  psum     Sum a stream of numbers
  pavg     Average a stream of numbers
  pmed     Median (with min / max) of a stream of numbers
  puniq    Shows only unique lines, without using sort, keeps the order


INSTALL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Clone the repository, make the scripts executable, and put them somewhere on
your PATH:

    git clone https://github.com/cade-vs/txfu.git
    cd txfu
    chmod +x grepw pcut psum pavg pmed
    cp grepw pcut psum pavg pmed ~/bin/      # or /usr/local/bin


DEPENDENCIES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * psum, pavg, pmed, pcut -- pure Perl, no non-core modules required.
  * grepw -- requires Data::Tools (used for recursive directory walking):

        cpanm Data::Tools


==============================================================================
  grepw
==============================================================================

Find a set of words in a file or in lines. Unlike plain grep, grepw prints a
line only when it matches EVERY word given -- the words act as an AND of
patterns, in any order. Each word is treated as a regular expression.

  usage: grepw <options> word word(s) ... -- file file(s)...

Options:

    -v        verbose, use first for full info
    -d        debug mode (repeatable)
    -r        recursive, descend into directories
    --        end of options and words; files/dirs follow
    -h        this help
    --help    the same

  * Search words and target files/directories are separated by "--".
  * The separator may also be written as "+", "/", or "---" (two or more
    dashes).
  * Options cannot be grouped: -dr is invalid -- use -d -r.
  * Binary files are skipped automatically (lines containing NUL).

Output is one line per match, in the form:

    filename:lineno<TAB>matching line

Examples:

    # lines containing BOTH "error" and "timeout"
    grepw error timeout -- /var/log/syslog

    # both words, anywhere in the C++ sources
    grepw debug print / *.cpp *.h

    # recurse a tree, lines with both TODO and FIXME
    grepw -r TODO FIXME -- src/

    # his classic
    grepw power button + /etc/ /usr/etc/


==============================================================================
  pcut
==============================================================================

A more capable cut. Splits each line on a delimiter and prints selected
fields, with optional reordering, ranges, sorting, head/tail, and a sample
mode for discovering column numbers.

  usage: pcut <options> <input files, or nothing to read stdin>

Options:

    -f fields   fields to print, e.g. -f 1,5,7-11,2
    -0          0-based field indexes (default starts at 1, like cut)
    -d regexp   delimiter as a regular expression (default: whitespace, \s+)
    -D delim    delimiter as a fixed string (auto-escaped)
    -j jstr     join string between output fields (default: TAB)
    -J          use the visual join string ' <|> '
    -p          strip leading/trailing whitespace from each line first
    -k          skip blank (whitespace-only) INPUT lines
    -k -k       given twice, also skip blank OUTPUT lines
    -s fields   sort output by these fields (may differ from -f)
    -r          reverse the sort
    -a          sample: show an ASCII table of the first lines with indexes
    -e N        head: output only the first N lines
    -t N        tail: output only the last N lines
    -i N        skip (ignore) the first N input lines
    -n          no-op: print input unmodified
    --          end of options; remaining arguments are filenames
    -h, --help  this help

Field syntax
............

-f (and -s) take a comma-separated list of field numbers and ranges:

    5,1-3,9     ->  fields 5, 1, 2, 3, 9
    1-3         ->  fields 1, 2, 3   (dash or .. / ... both work: 1..3)
    15-         ->  field 15 to end  (open-ended; capped at 1024)

Indexes start at 1 by default (as in cut); pass -0 to count from 0.

Sample mode
...........

-a reads the first few lines, splits them, and prints a bordered table with a
header row of column indexes -- handy for finding the field numbers you want
without retyping the command. A later -e N (placed after -a) changes how many
lines are sampled.

Examples
........

    # PID and full command from ps (field 2, then 11 to end)
    ps aux | pcut -f 2,11-

    # username and login shell from /etc/passwd (colon-delimited)
    pcut -D : -f 1,7 /etc/passwd

    # discover columns in a messy file
    pcut -a data.txt

    # first three CSV columns, joined with " | "
    pcut -D , -f 1-3 -j ' | ' data.csv

    # fields 1 and 3, sorted by field 3, reversed
    pcut -f 1,3 -s 3 -r data.txt


==============================================================================
  psum / pavg / pmed
==============================================================================

Three tiny stream reducers. Each reads numbers from standard input or from
file arguments (one number per line works best), ignores lines that contain
no digits, and prints a single summary line of space-separated columns.

  psum -- sum:

      <sum>   <count>   <lines>

  pavg -- average:

      <average>   <sum>   <count>   <lines>

  pmed -- median, with extremes:

      <median>   <min>   <max>   <count>   <lines>

In every case "count" is the number of numeric lines used and "lines" is the
total number of input lines read (the difference tells you how many
non-numeric lines were skipped).

Examples:

    # sum the third whitespace field of a log
    pcut -f 3 access.log | psum

    # average of 1..100  ->  50.5 ...
    seq 1 100 | pavg

    # median response time straight from a file
    pmed times.txt

Notes:

  * Feed one number per line; each line is taken in numeric context, so a
    leading number is what counts.
  * pavg divides by the numeric count -- an input with no numeric lines will
    fail with a division-by-zero error.
  * For an even number of values, pmed returns the upper of the two middle
    values rather than their average.

==============================================================================
  puniq
==============================================================================

Prints unique lines only but keeps the order and does not use sorting.
It uses lookup memory and can produce only first or last seen lines.
There is an option to map lines to sha1 lookups to reduce used lookup
memory for data with long lines. However sha1 may increase used memory for
short lines and in all cases will use more cpu.

Examples:

    # print unique messages in the order they are last seen:
    puniq /var/log/messages -l

    # /var/log/messages tend to have longer lines so reduce lookup memory:
    puniq /var/log/messages -l -1


NOTES & CURRENT LIMITATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A few pcut options behave differently from the embedded help, or depend on
others, in the current version:

  * -e (head) and -t (tail) take effect only when -s (sort) is also given;
    without -s the output is produced in a streaming pass that does not trim.
  * Sorting is string comparison (with -r to reverse). A numeric sort is not
    currently selectable.
  * -d is consumed as the regexp-delimiter option, so it does not toggle
    debug output as the help text suggests.
  * -n selects no-op passthrough (print input unmodified).

These are worth keeping in mind until the option handling is reworked.


